#use(std/rfc2119)
#use(std/footnote)

#section{Scheduling protocol}::

#rfc2119-boilerplate

A scheduler #ref('must) reject a queue entry naming an unknown catalog
object and #ref('must-not) reorder entries of equal priority. It
#ref('should) prefer targets near culmination#note{Culmination is the moment
a target crosses the local meridian — the best airmass it will get.} and
#ref('may) split long exposures across nights; draft plans are
#ref('optional).

An #def("plan"){observation plan}{a night's ordered target list with start
times, recomputed whenever the queue changes} is the scheduler's only
output. The handbook's index referenced this #ref("plan") before the
definition existed — cross-references are order-independent, across
files.#note{The whole document set shares one info table during the collect
pass, which is also what keeps anchors and section numbers unique set-wide.}

